[2026-08-26 15:55:53] [INFO] MODEL RESPONSE: To prepare your home and family for an approaching hurricane, it is crucial to take a multi-faceted approach that includes securing your property, assembling an emergency kit, developing a family communication and evacuation plan, and staying informed. The best time to prepare is before hurricane season begins, typically June 1st.

**1. Home Preparation:**
*   **Secure your property:** Trim trees and shrubs around your home to prevent damage from falling branches. Clear loose and clogged rain gutters and downspouts.
*   **Windows and Doors:** Cover all windows and doors. Permanent storm shutters offer the best protection. Alternatively, you can board up windows with 5/8-inch marine plywood cut to fit. Taping windows does not prevent them from breaking.
*   **Loose Objects:** Bring inside or secure all outdoor furniture and any potential projectiles.
*   **Roof:** Consider installing straps or additional clips to securely fasten your roof to the frame structure to reduce damage.
*   **Utilities:** Locate your main electric fuse box, water service main, and natural gas main and learn how to turn them off. Turn off utilities only if you suspect lines are damaged or if instructed to do so. If you turn off gas, a professional will be needed to turn it back on. Otherwise, turn your refrigerator and freezer to their coldest settings and keep their doors closed.
*   **Water Management:** Use sandbags to redirect water away from your home and prevent water damage. You can also fill bathtubs and freeze bags of water in advance, as a storm can disrupt your water supply.
*   **Safe Room:** Identify a safe room inside your home, preferably at the lowest level, without windows, such as a bathroom, closet, or basement, to shelter in if you cannot evacuate.

**2. Family Preparation:**
*   **Emergency Kit (Go-Bag):** Assemble a kit with enough supplies for at least several days, ideally 7 to 10 days, for each person and pet in your household. Store this kit in a waterproof, easy-to-carry container. Essential items include:
    *   **Water:** One gallon per person per day for drinking and sanitation.
    *   **Food:** A supply of non-perishable food, along with a manual can opener. Consider special dietary needs, infant formula, and pet food.
    *   **Medications:** At least a two-week supply of prescription medications and any necessary medical supplies. Include a summary of medical history and shot records, especially for children with special needs.
    *   **First Aid Kit:** A well-stocked first aid kit.
    *   **Communication & Light:** Battery-powered or hand-crank radio, NOAA Weather Radio with tone alert, flashlights, and extra batteries. Cell phone chargers and power banks are also essential.
    *   **Sanitation & Hygiene:** Moist towelettes, garbage bags, plastic ties, soap, hand sanitizer, and feminine hygiene products.
    *   **Important Documents:** Copies of insurance policies, identification, birth certificates, marriage licenses, bank account records, and other critical papers in a waterproof container.
    *   **Cash:** ATMs and credit card machines may not work, so have cash on hand.
    *   **Tools:** Wrench or pliers to turn off utilities.
    *   **Comfort Items:** Books, games, or puzzles for children, and comfort items for both adults and children.
*   **Family Emergency Plan:**
    *   **Communication:** Decide how your family will contact each other, especially if separated. Choose an out-of-town friend or relative as a central contact person.
    *   **Meeting Places:** Establish a meeting place outside your home and another outside your neighborhood in case of evacuation.
    *   **Evacuation Plan:** Know if you live in an evacuation zone. Plan your evacuation route and destination in advance. Keep your car's gas tank full, especially if evacuation seems likely. If you must evacuate, do not delay once an order is given. Remember to take your emergency supply kit and pets with you.
    *   **Special Needs:** Make arrangements for family members with special needs, including infants, the elderly, those with disabilities, and pets. Local health departments may register families requiring special needs shelters.
    *   **Involve Children:** Include children in preparedness conversations and have them help make the emergency kit. Teach them emergency numbers.
    *   **Practice:** Conduct drills and quiz your family on the plan every six months.

**3. Stay Informed:**
*   **Monitor Alerts:** Pay attention to local media, NOAA Weather Radio, and Wireless Emergency Alerts (WEA) for up-to-date weather information, evacuation details, and shelter locations.
*   **FEMA App:** Download the FEMA App for severe weather alerts and safety tips.

By taking these steps, you can significantly enhance your family's safety and your home's resilience against an approaching hurricane.
